## Checkpoint 1: cap pairing and the refill boundary

The September-25 pairing proof survives its stated asymptotic scope: a
macroscopic actual cap forces a distinct antipodal cap with equal leading
area/population. Each isolate hull is O(sqrt(D)) from its own cap; inter-cap
antipodality is only o(D), not O(sqrt(D)). No exact finite symmetry is imported.

New candidate: any finite packet in one or several separated exterior open
half-planes, with all involving upper distances strict, can have *every*
new contact opened. Outward translation opens new-old contacts; tiny dilation
opens new-new contacts. Thus exact contacts confined to cap packets cannot
pay an interior replacement of any contact-active core label. This includes
packets initially containing triangles. Crossing a closing chord or moving
the retained source is necessary for that mechanism.

New candidate composition: occupied cap -> no loaded-only KKT stress -> a
stress transversal of <=s_Z+1 unloaded core labels -> strict reduced-source
diameter descent with all old diameter endpoints and all isolates retained.
The deletion/transversal face argument is prior work from PR #722; the new
scope extension uses canonical audit.62 adjacency. Cardinality restoration
remains unproved and cannot be obtained by the exterior-only packets above.

## Checkpoint 2: an exact global paired-cap comparison

The new spoke-frame family has actual six-vertex loaded hulls, three actual
diameters, positive weights on all core contacts/diameters, six paired
quadratic caps, O(D) slack, and exact depth-one cap coverage by actual
isolates. Its discrete lattice guard injection gives enough sites at the
same original cardinality in a radius-(D/2-1/4) disk, hence diameter <=D-1/2.
It is explicitly nonglobal and fails mixed rigidity and the canonical
linear diameter population. It does not establish the canonical vacancy
injection. The full finite M=8 instance and five exact validations are saved.

## Checkpoint 3: the cap base cannot supply the missing contact payment

E.6 extends the finite packet barrier to every inserted site outside
